{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/language.rs",
  "item": "fn[whole_comment]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This callback fills the Profile interface for every non-Rust profile.\n Those profiles all set hashes_comment to never, so Builder::encode skips\n comment_prose for every source input; plain text does not use the grammar\n encoder at all. Rust hashes doc comments through its separate doc_prose.\n The fallback therefore has no reachable behavior in the supported profiles."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/language.rs#fn[whole_comment]` is a fenced justification on an explicit function, which the built-in allows. Its prose agrees with the current source.\n\n- Every non-Rust profile sets `comment_prose` to `whole_comment`. These are css, markdown, python, toml, typescript and yaml in `src/outline/languages/*.rs#static[PROFILE]` (typescript's is `const[TYPESCRIPT]`), plus plain text in `src/outline/plain.rs#static[PROFILE]`.\n- All of those profiles set `hashes_comment: never`.\n- `src/outline/mod.rs#impl[Builder<'tree, 'source>]/fn[encode]` is the only place `comment_prose` is called. It calls it only when `hashes_comment` returns true, so `whole_comment` is unreachable for those profiles.\n- Plain text has `language: None`, so it doesn't use the grammar encoder.\n- Rust uses `hashes_comment: is_doc_comment` with its own `comment_prose: doc_prose` in `src/outline/languages/rust.rs`, not `whole_comment`.\n\nThe reason is substantive: it explains why the fallback has no reachable behavior, rather than saying it is hard to cover. No gap found.",
